# send2ereader configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Every setting is optional. With no .env at all the app comes up working:
# anyone can send a book with a 4-character key, and the first person to create
# an account becomes the admin and can configure the rest from the browser.
# Uncomment only what you actually want to change.
#
# Real environment variables always win over this file, so `docker run -e ...`
# and compose `environment:` entries override anything set here.
#
# Values must NOT be quoted: `docker run --env-file` takes quotes literally
# and would include them in the value.
#
# Most of what is here can also be set from the admin page, by the first
# account. A value set there is stored in the database and beats this file;
# Reset on the field deletes it and this file applies again. This file is
# never rewritten. LOCKED_SETTINGS, below, pins a key so the page cannot
# touch it.
#
# Every key in this file appears on the admin page, but the ones that decide
# how the process starts or what protects it are shown there read-only: the
# listen address and port, every directory, DB_PATH, the converter binaries,
# SESSION_SECRET, SCRYPT_N, LOCKED_SETTINGS, the log settings and ACCOUNTS.
# Those are changed here and take a restart. Everything else can be edited
# from the page and most of it takes hold immediately.


# ── Server ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the server listens. These are the inside of the box and never appear
# in a link.
# HTTP_PORT=3001
# HTTP_ADDR=0.0.0.0
# LOG_LEVEL=info
# Legacy switch, kept because deployments set it. LOG_FORMAT replaces it and
# wins when both are set; LOG_PRETTY=false still means json.
# LOG_PRETTY=false
#
# text is readable and the default. json is one structured line per event,
# which is what a log collector wants. Flag: --log-format=text|json
# LOG_FORMAT=text
#
# How many conversions one client address may start per window, and how long
# that window is. The default is sized for people; a test rig that converts in
# a loop wants it raised rather than the limiter switched off, because the
# limiter is what stops one client occupying every converter.
# CONVERT_RATE_MAX=5
# CONVERT_RATE_WINDOW=1 minute
#
# Only log these scopes, comma separated: server, http, convert, and the
# converter names. A name prefixed with - is excluded instead. Empty is
# everything. Flag: --log-scope=convert,-layoutfix
# LOG_SCOPE=
#
# iso is date, clock and UTC offset. short drops the date, rel counts seconds
# from start, none leaves it out. Flag: --log-time=iso|short|rel|none
# LOG_TIME=iso
#
# Set to anything and the log is never coloured, whatever --log-color says.
# The convention is not ours: see no-color.org.
# NO_COLOR=
# Honour X-Forwarded-* — set when running behind a reverse proxy, so client
# IPs are logged and rate limited correctly.
# TRUST_PROXY=false


# ── Sending ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MAX_FILE_SIZE=838860800        # 800 MB
# KEY_LENGTH=4
# The grace a key gets once its ereader stops answering. Every poll pushes the
# deadline this far out, so while the device is there the key does not expire
# at all and the clock only starts when it goes quiet. Extend on the sending
# side restarts the same timer — it does not add to what is left.
# EXPIRE_SECONDS=300             # 5 minutes
# The total a key may live, counted from when it was minted. No poll and no
# Extend can push this out, so a key page left open in a tab cannot hold one
# open indefinitely. Keep it above EXPIRE_SECONDS or the idle grace never gets
# to run: at the default the key gets its five minutes of reconnect and still
# dies ten minutes after it was made.
# MAX_EXPIRE_SECONDS=600         # 10 minutes, hard TTL, never extended
#
# How often the key page asks after its key. The sender is told "Connection
# lost" after three missed polls, so this sets how quickly a device going
# offline shows up — at the cost of requests from a battery-powered e-ink
# device. download.html carries the same number and a test pins them together.
# EREADER_POLL_SECONDS=2
# LAYOUT_FIX_DEFAULT=true        # "Fix EPUB layout" checkbox default
#
# The Convert page hands its result back as a download and deletes the file as
# it serves it. This is only the backstop for a reader who converts a book and
# then closes the tab, so nothing is left on disk.
# CONVERT_TTL=1800               # 30 minutes

# Where uploads are staged while a key is alive. The Docker image defaults
# this to /data/uploads, inside the mounted volume.
# The volume everything that persists lives under: the database, the library,
# and anything an extension installs. Changing it means changing the mount.
# DATA_DIR=/data
#
# UPLOAD_DIR=uploads
# Wipe leftover uploads at startup. Turn off only if UPLOAD_DIR is shared.
# CLEAN_UPLOAD_DIR_ON_BOOT=true
# STATIC_DIR=static


# ── Keeping books (off by default) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Everything else here is an outbox: uploads/ and queue/ are wiped on boot and
# a book is deleted the moment it is delivered. Retention is that behaviour
# deliberately withdrawn, for signed-in readers who ask for it, and it is the
# only store in this server that survives a restart.
#
# With RETAIN_DAYS at 0 nothing is ever written: no file, no row, no library.
# Above 0 it is both the default and the ceiling — an account may choose fewer
# days in Settings, or none at all, but never more. Each book carries its own
# deadline, so lowering this never retroactively deletes what was already kept.
# RETAIN_DAYS=0
# LIBRARY_DIR=library            # /data/library in the Docker image
#
# Both caps belong to the operator and no account can override them: keeping
# books is a convenience granted, and the disk it lands on is not the reader's
# to spend. Filling a per-user share is something a reader can fix by deleting
# books; filling the server total is not, so the page says the server is out of
# space rather than blaming them, and the book is delivered without being kept.
# STORAGE_PER_USER=1073741824    # 1 GiB per account
# STORAGE_TOTAL=10737418240      # 10 GiB across the whole library


# ── Accounts (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Accounts are ON. The login pages appear and a small SQLite database is
# created; none of it changes how sending works, which never needs an account.
# Turn them off and this is the bare key-transfer app, with no sign-in and no
# admin page — and so nothing to configure it with, which is why it is set
# here and nowhere else.
# ACCOUNTS=true
#
# Signs every session and encrypts stored Kobo tokens and two-factor secrets.
# Leave it unset and one is generated on first boot and written next to the
# database as session.key, mode 0600 — deleting that file signs everyone out
# and makes what it encrypted unreadable. Set it here and that file is never
# made, which is the better answer when several instances share a database.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# SESSION_SECRET=

# How people actually reach this server, which is not how it listens. These two
# build every link the server hands out: the ones in e-mails, the endpoint a
# Kobo is given, and the site a passkey belongs to.
#
# Set them to whatever answers in front — a reverse proxy, usually, since this
# server speaks http only. Leave DOMAIN unset and the address is built from
# HTTP_ADDR and HTTP_PORT instead, which is right when nothing sits in front.
#
# PROTOCOL also decides whether the session cookie is marked secure.
#
# Passkeys are fussy about DOMAIN in two ways: browsers only allow WebAuthn on
# https or localhost, and they will not accept an IP address as the site a
# passkey belongs to — it has to be a domain name. So send.example.com works and
# 192.168.1.10 does not, whatever certificate is in front of it. Two-factor,
# passwords and sign-in links work either way.
# PROTOCOL=http
# DOMAIN=send.example.com

# The first account to register becomes the administrator. Leave this false
# unless you want anyone who can reach the page to register.
# ALLOW_SIGNUP=false

# Where the accounts database lives. The Docker image defaults this to
# /data/db/send2ereader.db, inside the mounted volume.
# DB_PATH=data/send2ereader.db

# How long a session lasts, and how long confirmation and password-reset
# links stay valid, in seconds.
#
# SESSION_TTL is an idle timeout: it is renewed every time the account is used,
# so it means "signed out after this long away", not "signed out this long
# after signing in". It is also the ceiling on "Stay signed in on this device" —
# ticking that box gets a cookie lasting exactly this long, leaving it unticked
# gets one that dies when the browser does, and nothing a reader can do goes
# past this number. Lower it and everyone's sessions shorten with it.
# SESSION_TTL=2592000            # 30 days
#
# ALLOW_STAY_SIGNED_IN=false removes the choice entirely: the box disappears
# from the sign-in pages and every session ends when the browser window does,
# whatever a request asks for. For a shared or public machine.
# ALLOW_STAY_SIGNED_IN=true
# EMAIL_TOKEN_TTL=86400          # 24 hours; confirmation and password-reset links
#
# Once SMTP is on, every account that never confirmed its address is asked to,
# and this is how many times it may answer "not now". After that the only ways
# past are to confirm the address or to change it. 0 asks once and means it.
# VERIFY_REMINDER_LIMIT=5
#
# A sign-in link is shorter-lived on purpose: it *is* the credential, since
# anyone who opens it is signed in, where a reset link still asks for a new
# password first. Every message states its own lifetime from these values, so
# changing one changes what the mail says.
# SIGNIN_LINK_TTL=900            # 15 minutes

# After this many wrong passwords in a row on one account, the address is sent a
# notice and pointed at a password reset. A right password clears the run, and
# only one notice goes out per ALERT_EVERY however long the guessing lasts, so
# this cannot be turned into a way to flood somebody's inbox.
#
# Nothing is locked: the account still opens for whoever knows the password.
#
# Keep BEFORE_ALERT under ten. Sign-in is rate limited to ten tries per five
# minutes, and attempts turned away by the limiter never reach the check, so a
# higher threshold would simply never be met.
# FAILED_SIGNINS_BEFORE_ALERT=5
# FAILED_SIGNIN_WINDOW=900       # 15 minutes; a slower run than this is not a run
# FAILED_SIGNIN_ALERT_EVERY=3600 # 1 hour between notices for the same account

# Password policy. Length beats composition rules, which is why the composition
# rules are all off and the length is not short. Every one of these can also be
# set from the admin page, where it overrides whatever is here.
# MIN_PASSWORD_LENGTH=10
# MAX_PASSWORD_LENGTH=1024
# PASSWORD_REQUIRE_UPPER=false
# PASSWORD_REQUIRE_LOWER=false
# PASSWORD_REQUIRE_DIGIT=false
# PASSWORD_REQUIRE_SYMBOL=false

# Keys the admin page must show read-only. Whatever is set here for a listed key
# wins, and the page cannot override it. Comma or space separated. Use this when
# admin is held by somebody other than whoever owns the machine.
# LOCKED_SETTINGS=OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET, SMTP_PASSWORD
# MAX_PASSWORD_LENGTH=1024

# scrypt cost. The default is OWASP-grade and takes roughly 130ms and 64MB
# per attempt. It is recorded inside each stored hash, so raising it later
# does not invalidate existing passwords. Lower it only on weak hardware.
# SCRYPT_N=65536


# ── SMTP (disabled by default) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# While disabled, confirmation and password-reset links are written to the
# server log instead of being sent:
#   docker logs send2ereader | grep /auth/verify
#
# Port 587 uses STARTTLS, 465 uses implicit TLS; both are handled from
# SMTP_PORT alone. Note that many providers (Gmail among them) rewrite or
# reject a From address that is not the authenticated account or a verified
# alias.
#
# SMTP_FROM_EMAIL is therefore optional: leave it unset and messages are sent
# from SMTP_USERNAME, which is the address most servers expect anyway. Set it
# only when the two genuinely differ — a relay account allowed to send as
# several addresses — or when the login is a bare name rather than an address.
SMTP_ENABLED=false                                # Enable outgoing email: true | false
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com                        # SMTP server hostname
# SMTP_PORT=587                                     # SMTP server port: 25 | 465 | 587
# SMTP_USERNAME=                                    # SMTP login username
# SMTP_PASSWORD=                                    # SMTP login password
# SMTP_FROM_EMAIL=                                  # Sender; defaults to SMTP_USERNAME
# SMTP_FROM_NAME=Send to eReader                    # Sender display name
# SMTP_SECURITY=starttls                            # starttls (587, 25) | ssl (465) | none
#
# SMTP_TLS is the older spelling and is still read when SMTP_SECURITY is unset:
# true becomes ssl on port 465 and starttls anywhere else, false becomes none.
# Setting SMTP_SECURITY makes SMTP_TLS irrelevant.
# SMTP_TLS=true                                     # Legacy. Prefer SMTP_SECURITY.
# SMTP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30                           # SMTP connection timeout
#
# With SMTP off, messages go to the log so a mail-less server is still usable,
# but the sign-in and reset links in them are live credentials and a log is not
# a mailbox — so they are redacted. Turn this on for a first run on your own
# machine, when reading the link out of the log is the point.
# MAIL_LOG_SECRETS=false                            # Log sign-in and reset links in full: true | false


# ── Registered Kobo devices ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# A registered Kobo pulls books into its own library instead of downloading
# them in its browser. Needs accounts, and DOMAIN so the endpoint URL
# handed to the device is reachable.
#
# A queued book waits here until the device syncs, then the file is deleted.
# Nothing is persisted: a restart clears the queue and purges the directory.
# KOBO_QUEUE_DIR=queue           # /data/queue in the Docker image
# KOBO_QUEUE_TTL=21600           # 6 hours; a Kobo may not sync for a while
# Anything built from the device's api_endpoint that this server does not
# implement is forwarded to the real store. Most store features are unaffected
# either way: the device's own config gives them absolute Kobo URLs, so they
# never come here. Turn the proxy off per device in the UI and it loses only
# the handful that do.
# KOBO_STORE_URL=https://storeapi.kobo.com
# Pointing a device here also takes over its image host, so the covers of books
# bought from Kobo arrive here too and are redirected back to this address.
# It is the host an untouched Kobo eReader.conf names; change it only if Kobo
# moves its images.
# KOBO_IMAGE_BASE_URL=https://cdn.kobo.com/book-images
# KOBO_PROXY_TIMEOUT_MS=10000    # a store outage must not hang the device
# KOBO_PROXY_BODY_LIMIT=1048576  # store calls are small


# ── Single sign-on ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# An additional way to sign in, alongside local accounts. Needs DOMAIN set,
# because the provider redirects back to <PROTOCOL>://<DOMAIN>/auth/sso/callback —
# register exactly that as the redirect URI at your IdP.
#
# OIDC_CONFIG_URL takes either the discovery document or the plain issuer URL.
# An SSO identity is only joined to an existing local account when the provider
# confirms the address. Leave OIDC_ADMIN_GROUP empty and SSO users are never
# made administrators.
OIDC_ENABLED=false                                # Enable SSO login via OpenID Connect: true | false
# OIDC_CONFIG_URL=https://your-idp/.well-known/openid-configuration    # OIDC discovery URL
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=                                   # OIDC client ID
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=                               # OIDC client secret
# OIDC_ADMIN_GROUP=                                 # OIDC group that grants admin privileges
# OIDC_PROVIDER_NAME=SSO                            # Name on the button: "Continue with Authentik"


# ── Converters ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Binary names, only needed if they are not on PATH under these names.
# KEPUBIFY_BIN=kepubify
# EBOOK_CONVERT_BIN=ebook-convert
# PDFCROPMARGINS_BIN=pdfcropmargins
# EPUB_LAYOUT_FIX_BIN=epub-layout-fix
# CALIBRE_CUSTOMIZE_BIN=calibre-customize
#
# Read by the epub-layout-fix wrapper itself rather than by this server, which is
# why it is not in src/config.ts. It points at the calibre plugin directory the
# wrapper imports. Unset, the wrapper falls back to /opt/epub-layout-fix, which
# is where the image puts the files, so the image has no need to set it.
# scripts/dev.ps1 does set it, for a checkout of the plugin next door.
# EPUB_LAYOUT_FIX_LIB=/opt/epub-layout-fix

# calibre --output-profile for Kindle targets. Set empty to omit the flag.
# CALIBRE_OUTPUT_PROFILE=kindle_pw3

# Why a downloaded book shows its cover on a Kindle. calibre otherwise stamps a
# random UUID into the file as an ASIN and marks it "you bought this", so the
# Kindle asks Amazon for a cover that does not exist and then refuses to use the
# one inside the file. This omits both markers. The only cost is that the Kindle
# will not sync your reading position across devices, which never worked for a
# sideloaded book anyway. Turn it off to get calibre's stock behaviour back.
# KINDLE_SHARE_NOT_SYNC=true
# Wall-clock cap for one conversion, in milliseconds.
# CONVERSION_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
# How much converter output to keep for error messages, in bytes.
# CONVERSION_OUTPUT_LIMIT=65536
# How many books may be converted at once. calibre will take a core and up to
# CONVERSION_TIMEOUT_MS over a single book, and /convert needs no account, so
# this is what stops one caller taking the machine. Anyone past the limit is
# turned away with a 503 rather than queued.
# CONVERSION_CONCURRENCY=2


# ── Extensions ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Things the image deliberately does not carry, installed at container start by
# whoever wants them. Both are pipe separated, the way linuxserver spells it.
#
# Each name is an image whose layers are unpacked over the filesystem; anything
# it leaves in /etc/s2e/extensions is then run once as root, before the server
# starts and before it drops to the node user. An extension that fails is logged
# and skipped rather than stopping the container. See docker/extensions.
#
# The one in this repo installs Wine and Amazon's Kindle Previewer, which is
# what writing KFX needs and what nobody may redistribute — so it is fetched on
# your machine, at your instruction, and never in the published image.
# EXTENSIONS=calibre|pdfcrop|kfx
#
# Debian packages, installed with apt before the server starts. Convenient, and
# paid for on every start; anything permanent belongs in an image of your own.
# EXTENSION_PACKAGES=fonts-noto-cjk|poppler-utils
#
# calibre's KFX Output plugin is in the image, but a plugin alone cannot write
# KFX: it drives Amazon's Kindle Previewer, which is a Windows program. So KFX
# is offered only when a Previewer is actually there. The kfx extension records
# the path of the one it installed and nothing needs setting. Point this at a
# Previewer that came from somewhere else if you have one.
# KFX_PREVIEWER_PATH=


# ── Advanced ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Load a different file instead of ./.env. Must be set as a real environment
# variable — it is read before any file is loaded.
# ENV_FILE=
